for(const [name,day,definition,answer,code,num] of sql){const reviews=name==='JOINs'?[32,42]:day===12?[34,37,44]:day===14?[34,37,44]:[39];const days=[day,...reviews];const problem=lc.get(num);const body=`<h2>Core Concepts</h2><p>${esc(definition)}</p><h2>Interview Questions and Scripts</h2>${qa(`How do you use ${name} correctly?`,answer,'What input would expose an incorrect assumption?')}${qa('What row grain and duplicate behavior do you expect?','I define one output row before writing the query, inspect key uniqueness on both sides of each join, and test duplicate inputs. DISTINCT is not a general repair for an incorrect join.','Could a one-to-many join inflate a financial total?')}${qa('How do nulls and ties affect this query?','I specify whether missing values should be retained, rejected or imputed. I use a deterministic tie breaker where one row is required and preserve ties when the business question asks for distinct ranks.','What happens to an unmatched row after a WHERE filter?')}${qa('How would you test and optimize the query?','I build a tiny fixture with known expected rows, including duplicates and missing values. After correctness, I inspect EXPLAIN or Query Profile on representative data; runtime depends on cardinality, indexes and the execution plan.','Would the same optimization apply to Snowflake and PostgreSQL?')}${qa('How does this matter in a financial analytics assistant?',answer+' I compare generated SQL results with an independently checked reference, not only with a matching SQL string.','How do you distinguish data-quality failure from query-generation failure?')}<h2>Representative Practice</h2><p><a href="${problem.url}">LeetCode ${num}: ${esc(problem.title)}</a> is a related candidate; the custom example below may use a different schema. Use the problem statement schema for submission.</p><p>Approach: ${esc(answer)}</p><p>Complexity: a scan is O(n); sorting is commonly O(n log n); index access and joins depend on the actual plan. Inspect rows scanned, intermediate cardinality and spills rather than asserting one universal bound.</p><p>Edge cases: empty tables, nulls, duplicate keys, tied values, zero denominators and missing dates.</p><details><summary>Reference SQL — reveal after your attempt</summary><pre><code>${esc(code)}</code></pre><p>Examples use PostgreSQL syntax unless the linked problem specifies another dialect. Tables are illustrative and must match your fixture; driver placeholders are not standalone SQL literals.</p></details><h2>Project Connection</h2><p>Use this concept in reference SQL for SEC facts or operational queries for experiment runs. The LLM proposes a query; database privileges and query limits enforce its execution scope.</p>`;const r=register(name,'SQL',days,body,'sql');days.forEach(d=>slots[d-1].coding.push(r));}
